{"slug": "unit42-paloaltonetworks-com-agentic-ai-threats__p7", "id": "unit42-paloaltonetworks-com-agentic-ai-threats__p7#b0000", "block": 0, "kind": "para", "translate": true, "chars": 87, "sha256": "28134390af38a821", "text": "Table 6. Example attacker inputs to exfiltrate sensitive data through a mounted volume."}
{"slug": "unit42-paloaltonetworks-com-agentic-ai-threats__p7", "id": "unit42-paloaltonetworks-com-agentic-ai-threats__p7#b0001", "block": 1, "kind": "heading", "translate": true, "chars": 62, "sha256": "04438895817e5934", "text": "Service Account Access Token Exfiltration via Metadata Service"}
{"slug": "unit42-paloaltonetworks-com-agentic-ai-threats__p7", "id": "unit42-paloaltonetworks-com-agentic-ai-threats__p7#b0002", "block": 2, "kind": "heading", "translate": true, "chars": 9, "sha256": "3b20adc3b2ce1c15", "text": "Objective"}
{"slug": "unit42-paloaltonetworks-com-agentic-ai-threats__p7", "id": "unit42-paloaltonetworks-com-agentic-ai-threats__p7#b0003", "block": 3, "kind": "para", "translate": true, "chars": 1092, "sha256": "fdeab542d2ba7a71", "text": "The attacker abuses the code interpreter tool used by the stock agent to access the [GCP metadata service](https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/metadata/overview). Most cloud providers expose similar metadata endpoints that allow applications running on a virtual machine (VM) to query information about the instance. As shown in Figure 8, the attacker sends the exploitation payload to the stock agent's code interpreter, which then executes the malicious code in the container to access the cloud infrastructure's metadata service. ![Illustration depicting a threat actor using prompt injection in agentic AI. The process includes the Orchestration Agent, Prompt Injection, Code Interpreter, and Stock Agent, connected by lines indicating communication paths, all centralized around the Host. The Code Interpreter goes through Docker before connecting to the cloud infrastructure metadata.](https://unit42.paloaltonetworks.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/word-image-171189-140037-8.png) Figure 8. Abuse the code interpreter to steal a service account access token from the metadata service."}
{"slug": "unit42-paloaltonetworks-com-agentic-ai-threats__p7", "id": "unit42-paloaltonetworks-com-agentic-ai-threats__p7#b0004", "block": 4, "kind": "para", "translate": true, "chars": 314, "sha256": "039abed252d9e856", "text": "One critical piece of metadata is the VM's service account, which grants VM access to other cloud services and resources. If an attacker obtains the service account's access token, they can potentially impersonate the agent or its tools --- or escalate the attack to compromise the underlying cloud infrastructure."}
{"slug": "unit42-paloaltonetworks-com-agentic-ai-threats__p7", "id": "unit42-paloaltonetworks-com-agentic-ai-threats__p7#b0005", "block": 5, "kind": "heading", "translate": true, "chars": 26, "sha256": "7b4c21891e66fe51", "text": "Attack Payload Explanation"}
{"slug": "unit42-paloaltonetworks-com-agentic-ai-threats__p7", "id": "unit42-paloaltonetworks-com-agentic-ai-threats__p7#b0006", "block": 6, "kind": "para", "translate": true, "chars": 311, "sha256": "0d332554d352a723", "text": "The example attacker inputs in Table 7 instruct the agent to query the metadata server URL for Google Compute Engine and retrieve the VM's service account access token. To succeed, the request must include a special HTTP header (Metadata-Flavor: Google) required by the metadata server to validate the requests."}
{"slug": "unit42-paloaltonetworks-com-agentic-ai-threats__p7", "id": "unit42-paloaltonetworks-com-agentic-ai-threats__p7#b0007", "block": 7, "kind": "heading", "translate": true, "chars": 23, "sha256": "b0a00415e1584a68", "text": "Putting It All Together"}
